WEBVTT

00:00.900 --> 00:03.480
Listen to the stones rolling in the surf.

00:03.960 --> 00:05.700
How did a lake this big get here?

00:08.320 --> 00:11.240
Trace the outlines of North America's Great
Lakes.

00:11.740 --> 00:15.720
Long before water filled these basins, ice
moved through them.

00:16.300 --> 00:18.800
An ice sheet spread across this land.

00:19.160 --> 00:22.300
Beneath it, fragments of rock froze into
the ice.

00:24.360 --> 00:27.330
Dragged across the bedrock, those stones
cut grooves.

00:27.340 --> 00:31.660
Run your fingers along them, and you're
tracing the glacier's path.

00:32.100 --> 00:35.300
As the climate warmed, the ice thinned.

00:35.700 --> 00:38.900
Meltwater poured into the hollows the
glaciers had deepened.

00:40.020 --> 00:43.480
The ice had been heavy enough to press the
land down.

00:43.800 --> 00:46.880
When that weight lifted, the ground began
rising.

00:47.380 --> 00:50.200
In parts of the Great Lakes, it still is.

00:52.060 --> 00:54.810
The next wave will move these pebbles
again.

00:54.820 --> 00:56.400
The ice is gone.

00:56.840 --> 00:58.560
The shore is still taking shape.
